727 / where swans were awakened by the tinkling of anklets; where lotuses were brushed by the swan-feathers; / where the pure water was reddened by lotus pollen; where the golden peaks were adorned by the water;
where sandalwood trees rose upon the peaks; where flocks of cuckoos perched upon the trees; / where trumpeting was uttered by herds of great elephants; where bears and hyenas were frightened by that roar;

where bears and hyenas lurked in bowers; where lions emerged from the mouths of cave-bowers; / where the frontal lobes of elephants were torn by lions; where pearls and jewels came forth from the frontal lobes; / where the ridges were illuminated by the rays of the gems; where the feet of Śaṅkara rested upon the ridges; / where groves of trees grew from the foot [of the mountain]; where pathways were covered with masses of flowers from clusters of creepers;
where every path was adorned with gemmed archways; where the lintels of the archways were fashioned of diverse precious stones; / where flowers were arranged upon every lintel; where every flower was kissed by bees; / whose slopes were variegated with various minerals; charming with diverse flower-bower dwellings; / resounding with the cries of diverse intoxicated birds; with Siddhas dwelling on its diverse peaks;
That Mount Mandara, delightful with various assemblies of Cāraṇas and Siddhas, where the Three-eyed Lord himself had made his dwelling— / all those Daityas, granted entrance by the Lord and desiring victory for themselves, then climbed it.
